# Logical Operators

Exactly like Python and C++.

| Python | C++ | JavaScript |
|---------|------|------------|
| and | && | && |
| or | || | || |
| not | ! | ! |

---

# Logical AND (&&)

Returns true only if every condition is true.

```javascript
const loggedIn = true;
const isAdmin = true;

console.log(loggedIn && isAdmin);
```

Output

```
true
```

---

Example

```javascript
true && false
```

Output

```
false
```

---

Real-world example

```javascript
if (loggedIn && hasPermission) {

    console.log("Access Granted");

}
```

---

# Logical OR (||)

Only one condition needs to be true.

```javascript
console.log(true || false);
```

Output

```
true
```

---

Example

```javascript
const isAdmin = false;
const isOwner = true;

if (isAdmin || isOwner) {

    console.log("Allowed");

}
```

---

# Logical NOT (!)

Reverses a Boolean value.

```javascript
console.log(!true);
```

Output

```
false
```

---

```javascript
console.log(!false);
```

Output

```
true
```

---

# Short-Circuit Evaluation

JavaScript stops evaluating expressions as soon as the result is known.

---

## AND (&&)

```javascript
false && anything
```

JavaScript immediately returns

```
false
```

because the whole expression can never become true.

---

## OR (||)

```javascript
true || anything
```

JavaScript immediately returns

```
true
```

because one true value is enough.

---

# Practical Example

Instead of

```javascript
if(user !== null){

    console.log(user.name);

}
```

Modern JavaScript often uses short-circuiting:

```javascript
user && console.log(user.name);
```

If `user` is `null`, the second part is never executed.

---

# Using || for Default Values

Example

```javascript
let username = "";

let displayName = username || "Guest";

console.log(displayName);
```

Output

```
Guest
```

This works because an empty string is a falsy value.

> **Note:** In modern JavaScript, the nullish coalescing operator (`??`) is often a better choice when you only want to replace `null` or `undefined`. We'll cover it in a later module.

---

# Operator Precedence

```javascript
true || false && false
```

JavaScript evaluates

```
&&

before

||
```

Always use parentheses when the expression becomes difficult to read.

---

# Common Mistakes

## Mistake 1

Using

```javascript
==
```

instead of

```javascript
===
```

---

## Mistake 2

Comparing different types unintentionally.

```javascript
5 == "5"
```

Avoid relying on automatic type conversion.

---

## Mistake 3

Writing long Boolean expressions without grouping.

Instead of

```javascript
a && b || c && d
```

Prefer

```javascript
(a && b) || (c && d)
```

This makes the intent much clearer.

